The discipline that prevents all of this: **the code that subscribes returns the code that unsubscribes**, so the two can never drift apart.

```js
// Vanilla: one function owns setup and teardown together
function mountRoute(el) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const { signal } = controller;

  // AbortController removes every listener registered with the signal at once
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize, { signal });
  document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey, { signal });

  const timer = setInterval(poll, 5000);
  const io = new IntersectionObserver(onSeen);
  const socket = new WebSocket(WS_URL);

  return function unmount() {
    controller.abort();      // all signal-bound listeners, in one call
    clearInterval(timer);
    io.disconnect();
    socket.close();
  };
}
```

```jsx
// React: the effect's return value is the teardown
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { signal: controller.signal });
  const id = setInterval(poll, 5000);

  return () => {              // runs on unmount and before every re-run
    controller.abort();
    clearInterval(id);
  };
}, [routeId]);
```

`AbortController` is the underused piece here: passing a `signal` to `addEventListener` means one `abort()` removes every listener bound to it, and the same signal cancels in-flight `fetch` requests. It removes the whole class of "we removed three of the four listeners" bugs.

The rule to state in an interview: **if a function's return value isn't a teardown, the caller has no way to clean up.** Design the API so cleanup is impossible to forget rather than easy to remember.
